INT. FERRY GALLEY KITCHEN - EVENING

A steel counter, a rattling stockpot, windows fogged by the steam. The boat rolls gently.

PHILOMENA OKAFOR-BRANDT, seventies, the ferry's cook, stirs without looking. A YOUNG MAN, twenties, stands at the galley door, soaked, holding a small suitcase against his chest.

PHILOMENA
We're closed to the public.

YOUNG MAN
I'm not the public.

She turns. She sees his face. The ladle stays in her hand, dripping.

PHILOMENA
You have your father's jaw.

YOUNG MAN
That's what Mum said.

PHILOMENA
Sit.

He does. She ladles soup into a mug and sets it before him, her hand trembling for an instant over the rim.

PHILOMENA (CONT'D)
Name.

YOUNG MAN
Leo.

PHILOMENA
Leopold.

LEO
Leo.

PHILOMENA
(over him)
I was going to say it's a fine name.

LEO ^
You didn't know I existed.

PHILOMENA ^
I knew every day.

They both stop. They both stare into the soup. The ferry's horn sounds, low and long, and the cups on the shelf chime against their hooks.

Leo takes up the spoon, and Philomena sits down across from him at last. Their hands meet on the counter, and neither moves away.
